---
// The landing page. The copy, the plates, the stream widget and the hand-
// coloured code blocks are the ones the single-file site shipped; only the
// scaffolding around them moved when the site moved to Astro. Where the old
// file said something in a CSS comment, the comment came with it.
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Plate from '../components/Plate.astro';
import BrewChip from '../components/BrewChip.astro';
import hero from '../assets/plates/hero.png';
import contention from '../assets/plates/contention.png';
import theatre from '../assets/plates/theatre.png';
import listener from '../assets/plates/listener.png';
import mic from '../assets/plates/mic.png';
// The real popover, rendered by the app itself (--render-preview, the
// listening state) — the same shot the social card uses.
import popover from '../assets/popover.png';
import { version, releaseUrl } from '../lib/version';
---

<Base
	title="Sonocles — words, while you're still saying them"
	description="Turns your Mac's microphone into a live stream of text over WebSocket or SSE — word by word, about 180 milliseconds behind you, and nowhere else. What listens is up to you."
	card={{
		image: '/img/og.png',
		alt: 'An illustrated Greek philosopher speaking, sound arcs travelling to a scribe who is writing it all down and keeping up.',
		description:
			"Turns your Mac's microphone into a live stream of text over WebSocket or SSE — word by word, about 180 milliseconds behind you. What listens is up to you. On-device, free and open source.",
		twitterDescription:
			"Your Mac's microphone as a live text stream over WebSocket or SSE — word by word, ~180 ms behind you. What listens is up to you.",
	}}
>
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="hero">
			<span class="free">Free · Open source · MIT</span>
			<h1>Words, while you're <br /><em>still saying them.</em></h1>
			<p class="lede">Sonocles turns your Mac's microphone into a live stream of text — over <b>WebSocket</b> or <b>server-sent events</b>, word by word, about 180&nbsp;milliseconds behind you.</p>
			<Image
				class="hero-popover"
				src={popover}
				width={344}
				densities={[1, 2]}
				format="png"
				loading="eager"
				alt="The Sonocles menu bar popover, listening: a level meter, the last lines of a transcript ending — and this week we are looking at the two proposals that landed — with its lag, the engine picker on Parakeet 160 ms and a line on when to choose it, the endpoints with two clients connected, the Control API token with Copy and Rotate, and a strip reading sonocles 0.1.3 · Parakeet 160 ms · up 1h 12m."
			/>
			<p class="lede2"><b>What listens is up to you.</b> A teleprompter that keeps your pace, a cue that fires on the word, live captions, a bot that answers to its own name. It streams; you decide.</p>
			<p class="sub">Runs on the Neural Engine. Nothing leaves the machine.</p>

			<div class="cta">
				<a class="btn btn-primary" href="https://github.com/artisan-build/sonocles/releases/latest/download/Sonocles.dmg">Download for Mac</a>
				<a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/docs">Read the docs</a>
				<a class="btn btn-ghost" href="https://github.com/artisan-build/sonocles">Source on GitHub</a>
				<span class="meta">{version && <><a href={releaseUrl}>v{version}</a> · </>}Apple Silicon · macOS 14 or later</span>
			</div>

			<p class="brew"><span>or</span> <BrewChip cmd="brew install --cask artisan-build/tap/sonocles" /></p>

			<Plate
				class="hero-plate"
				src={hero}
				loading="eager"
				alt="Illustration: a cheerful bearded philosopher speaking, sound arcs sweeping from his mouth to a delighted scribe who is keeping up easily."
				caption="<b>The whole product, basically.</b> One person talks. Something else keeps up. The difficult part is the second half."
			/>

			<div class="stream" aria-label="Example event stream">
				<div class="row r1"><span>partial</span><span class="n">lag 120ms</span><span class="t">the</span></div>
				<div class="row r2"><span>partial</span><span class="n">lag 180ms</span><span class="t">the menu</span></div>
				<div class="row r3"><span>partial</span><span class="n">lag 200ms</span><span class="t">the menu bar</span></div>
				<div class="row r4"><span>partial</span><span class="n">lag 200ms</span><span class="t">the menu bar app</span></div>
				<div class="row r5"><span>partial</span><span class="n">lag 140ms</span><span class="t">the menu bar app is</span></div>
				<p class="cap">Real frames, about one word each. <span class="n">lag</span> is how far behind the speaker that word arrived.</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<Section kicker="Why it exists">
			<h2>It started as crackly audio.</h2>
			<p class="pull">The speech-follow built into the teleprompter was fighting the encoder for CPU. The prompter kept up. The audio did not.</p>
			<p class="lead">OBS wants every core it can get. So does a software encoder. Adding speech recognition to that same scrap meant dropped samples — and dropped samples on a take you cannot re-shoot are not a performance problem, they are a lost afternoon.</p>
			<p class="lead" style="margin-top:14px">The Neural Engine, meanwhile, was sitting there doing absolutely nothing. It is a separate piece of silicon that no encoder is competing for. Moving the listening onto it did not make it faster so much as make it <em>free</em>: it stopped taking anything the recording needed.</p>

			<Plate
				src={contention}
				alt="Illustration: two figures crammed at one small desk, elbows colliding, the scribe's stylus skidding off his tablet, while an identical empty desk sits beside them."
				caption="<b>Two craftsmen, one desk, and a perfectly good second desk.</b> Nobody is doing anything wrong. That is what makes it so annoying."
			/>
		</Section>

		<Section kicker="The distinction">
			<h2>It is not a dictation app.</h2>
			<p class="lead">Dictation hands you a sentence once you have finished saying it. Perfectly good, when you are writing and nothing downstream is waiting. Useless when something is: a teleprompter that has to keep your pace, a cue that has to fire on a word, an editor that needs to know when you said it rather than when the transcript turned up.</p>
			<div class="tablewrap">
			<table>
				<tr><th></th><th>Dictation</th><th>Sonocles</th></tr>
				<tr><td>Gives you text</td><td>when you stop talking</td><td>while you are still talking</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Aims at</td><td>a text field</td><td>a socket</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Latency that matters</td><td>none, really</td><td>all of it</td></tr>
				<tr><td>You are</td><td>writing</td><td>performing</td></tr>
			</table>
			</div>
			<p class="lead" style="margin-top:28px">If dictation <em>is</em> what you want, use <a href="https://www.sonari.audio/">Sonari</a>. It is genuinely good, it runs locally too, and it is made by a friend. We compared notes and concluded the use cases diverge: Sonari is built around the moment you stop speaking. This one is built around the moment you have not.</p>
		</Section>

		<Section kicker="The measurement">
			<h2>Word by word, not<br />sentence by sentence.</h2>
			<p class="lead">One instrument, one sentence, one microphone. Apple's own Speech framework delivers that sentence in four bursts, 3.7&nbsp;seconds apart. We ship the comparison so you can re-run it instead of believing us.</p>
			<div class="tablewrap">
			<table>
				<tr><th>Engine</th><th>Arrival gap</th><th>Behind live</th><th>Words per arrival</th></tr>
				<tr><td>Apple SpeechAnalyzer</td><td class="num">3747 ms</td><td class="num">0 ms, four times</td><td class="num">8.8</td></tr>
				<tr><td>Parakeet 320 ms</td><td class="num">302 ms</td><td class="num">540 ms</td><td class="num">2.3</td></tr>
				<tr class="win"><td>Parakeet 160 ms</td><td class="num">206 ms</td><td class="num">180 ms</td><td class="num">1.5</td></tr>
			</table>
			</div>
			<pre><span class="c"># the whole comparison, on your machine, one target</span>
make baseline</pre>
		</Section>

		<Section kicker="What it is for">
			<h2>Driving a prompter, mostly.</h2>
			<p class="lead">That is the honest answer. It exists because <a href="https://pteroprompter.com">Pteroprompter</a> needed it, and that is still what it is tuned for. But nothing about it is prompter-shaped — it streams words and timestamps at a socket, and what listens is your business.</p>
			<ul class="uses">
				<li><b>Speech-follow scrolling</b> — the script keeps your pace instead of a fixed rate you have to chase.</li>
				<li><b>Cue firing</b> — a control token triggers the instant you reach it, not a second and a half later.</li>
				<li><b>Editorial markers</b> — every frame carries the audio time it was spoken, so markers land on the words.</li>
				<li><b>Live captioning</b> — for a stream, a talk, or a room, with no cloud round trip.</li>
				<li><b>Anything with a socket</b> — a lighting cue, a slide advance, a bot that answers to its own name.</li>
			</ul>

			<Plate
				size="mid"
				src={theatre}
				alt="Illustration: a Greek amphitheatre seen from above as concentric rings radiating from one tiny figure on the central stage, reading equally as a sound wave."
				caption="<b>An amphitheatre, from above.</b> Concentric rings around one small figure — which is also, exactly, how you draw a voice leaving a mouth. The building is a diagram of what it is for. The mark is the same drawing with the back half left off, because a voice only goes one way."
			/>
		</Section>

		<Section kicker="What it does">
			<h2>Five things worth knowing.</h2>
			<div class="grid">
				<div class="card">
					<h3>It knows <em>when</em> you said it</h3>
					<p>Every frame carries the audio time it describes, not just the time it arrived. Arrival time drifts with the delivery schedule; audio time does not. That is the difference between a marker you can cut on and one you have to nudge.</p>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<h3>It does not want your CPU</h3>
					<p>The models run on the Neural Engine, which your encoder is not competing for. Not a benchmark boast — it is the entire reason the thing was written. Recognition that costs you cores costs you takes.</p>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<h3>It does not make things up</h3>
					<p>Thirty seconds of loud piano, peaking at <span class="fig-num">-9&nbsp;dBFS</span>, produced zero words. That is a measurement, not a promise: a streaming model can emit a spurious token, and some will confidently transcribe music into sentences, which is a genuine hazard when the output drives a scroll. This one stayed quiet.</p>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<h3>Nothing leaves your Mac</h3>
					<p>Models download once and run on device. The sockets bind to loopback only. There is no account, no key and no server — not as a policy, but because none was ever built.</p>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<h3>Anything can drive it</h3>
					<p>SSE and WebSocket, both live at once. An HTTP control API to start and stop it, behind a token your apps can read and a web page cannot. And a CLI that reports its own latency, because the numbers up there should be yours to check.</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</Section>

		<Section kicker="Using it">
			<h2>Point something at a socket.</h2>
			<pre><span class="c">// both transports carry identical JSON, both live at once</span>
<span class="k">const</span> es = <span class="k">new</span> EventSource('http://127.0.0.1:7357/events')
<span class="k">const</span> ws = <span class="k">new</span> WebSocket('ws://127.0.0.1:7358')</pre>
			<pre>&lbrace; <span class="k">"type"</span>: "partial", <span class="k">"text"</span>: "the menu bar",
  <span class="k">"audioStart"</span>: 40.28, <span class="k">"audioEnd"</span>: 40.8,
  <span class="k">"lagMs"</span>: 200, <span class="k">"seq"</span>: 3 &rbrace;</pre>
			<p class="note" style="margin-top:20px">Use <code>audioEnd</code>, not <code>ts</code>. A missing <code>lagMs</code> means unmeasured — never zero. We are quite firm about this. The rest is in <a href="/docs">the docs</a>.</p>
		</Section>

		<Section kicker="The name">
			<h2>Renowned for sound.</h2>
			<div class="two" style="margin-top:26px">
				<div>
					<p class="greek">κλέος</p>
					<p class="lead">The <span class="mono">-cles</span> in Sophocles and Pericles is the Greek <span class="mono">-κλῆς</span>, from <em>kleos</em>: renown. It comes down from a root meaning <b class="ink">to hear</b> — the same one that gives English the word <em>loud</em>. In Homer it is specifically the fame that survives because bards sing it and people hear it.</p>
					<p class="lead" style="margin-top:14px">So the suffix does not merely mean famous. It means <em>heard of</em>. We would love to claim that was the plan.</p>
					<p class="note" style="margin-top:14px"><b class="ink-soft">Sono-</b> is Latin, mind you. So the name is a seam between two languages, which is not scholarship so much as an accident we have decided to be pleased about. So is <em>television</em>. Nobody minds.</p>
				</div>
				<Plate
					size="sm"
					class="name-plate"
					src={listener}
					alt="Illustration: a round friendly bearded figure cupping an enormous ear, eyes squeezed shut in theatrical concentration, sound arcs arriving at his hand."
					caption="<b>Heard of.</b>"
				/>
			</div>
		</Section>

		<Section kicker="Anyway">
			<h2>Here is a Greek man<br />with a podcast.</h2>
			<p class="lead">He has been talking for some time. The small winged one has kept up with every word, roughly 180&nbsp;milliseconds behind, and has not once had to ask him to repeat himself.</p>
			<Plate
				src={mic}
				alt="Illustration: a relaxed bearded figure reclining with a wine cup, talking into a retro broadcast microphone, while a small winged figure behind him takes notes with intense concentration."
				caption="<b>Nobody in this picture finds any of it unusual.</b> Which is, in fairness, the goal."
			/>
		</Section>
	</div>
</Base>

<style>
	.hero {
		padding: 56px 0 20px;
	}
	/* Not a Plate: no caption, no box. A window floating over the page, so a
	   shadow and its own corners are all it gets. */
	.hero :global(.hero-popover) {
		display: block;
		width: 100%;
		max-width: 344px;
		height: auto;
		border-radius: 12px;
		box-shadow:
			0 1px 2px rgba(42, 33, 26, 0.12),
			0 12px 32px -8px rgba(42, 33, 26, 0.28);
	}
	/* Wide: copy left, the popover right, spanning the copy's rows; the plate
	   and the frames stay below, full width. Every hero child is a sibling so
	   the phone order (below) can move the popover under the first paragraph. */
	@media (min-width: 761px) {
		.hero {
			display: grid;
			grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 11fr) minmax(0, 9fr);
			grid-template-rows: repeat(6, auto) 1fr auto auto;
			column-gap: 2.5rem;
			align-items: start;
		}
		.hero > * {
			grid-column: 1;
		}
		.hero > .free {
			justify-self: start;
		}
		.hero > :global(.hero-popover) {
			grid-column: 2;
			grid-row: 1 / span 7;
			justify-self: end;
			margin-top: 0.4rem;
		}
		.hero > :global(.hero-plate),
		.hero > .stream {
			grid-column: 1 / -1;
		}
		/* The headline in a column: two authored lines, sized to fit them
		   (`:first-of-type` only to outrank the page-wide rule further down). */
		.hero > h1:first-of-type {
			font-size: 40px;
		}
	}
	/* At the page's full measure the popover takes a fixed 320px against the
	   column's right edge — level with the header's GitHub link. */
	@media (min-width: 1000px) {
		.hero {
			grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 320px;
		}
		.hero > :global(.hero-popover) {
			max-width: 320px;
		}
		.hero > h1:first-of-type {
			font-size: 56px;
		}
	}
	.free {
		display: inline-block;
		margin: 0 0 20px;
		font-size: 12px;
		font-weight: 600;
		letter-spacing: 0.09em;
		text-transform: uppercase;
		color: #fff;
		background: var(--terracotta-deep);
		border-radius: 999px;
		padding: 6px 15px;
	}
	.cta {
		display: flex;
		gap: 12px;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		align-items: center;
		margin: 30px 0 0;
	}
	.cta .meta {
		color: var(--script);
		font-size: 13.5px;
	}
	/* The version, read at build time (src/lib/version.ts): a fact, not a
	   badge — mono, the line's own colour, linked to that tag's release. */
	.cta .meta a {
		color: inherit;
		font-family: var(--mono);
		text-decoration-color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--script) 40%, transparent);
	}
	/* The second install route: BrewChip, the command in a chip with a Copy
	   button beside it. */
	.brew {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		gap: 10px;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		margin: 16px 0 0;
		font-size: 14px;
		color: var(--ink-faint);
	}
	/* The chip-and-button may shrink to the column; the "or" wraps above it
	   on a phone rather than the button under the chip. */
	.brew > :global(.homebrew) {
		flex: 0 1 auto;
		min-width: 0;
	}
	.hero h1 {
		font-size: clamp(40px, 7.6vw, 80px);
		font-weight: 600;
		letter-spacing: -0.022em;
		margin-bottom: 24px;
	}
	.hero h1 em {
		font-style: normal;
		color: var(--terracotta);
	}
	.lede {
		font-size: clamp(18px, 2.2vw, 22px);
		color: var(--ink-soft);
		max-width: 56ch;
		margin: 0 0 12px;
	}
	.lede2 {
		font-size: clamp(17px, 2vw, 20px);
		color: var(--ink-soft);
		max-width: 58ch;
		margin: 0 0 14px;
	}
	.lede2 b {
		color: var(--ink);
		font-weight: 600;
	}
	.sub {
		color: var(--ink-faint);
		max-width: 58ch;
		margin: 0;
		font-size: 16.5px;
	}

	/* Narrow screens.
	   The authored line breaks in the headline are a desktop nicety and a bug on
	   a phone: a forced break makes "Words, while you're" one unbreakable line,
	   which at this size is wider than the viewport, and the whole page scrolls
	   sideways. Let it wrap naturally instead.

	   The hero is also re-ordered so the illustration lands directly after the
	   one-sentence explanation. On a phone the hook, the what, and the picture
	   are what fit; the detail can follow underneath. Pure `order`, so the
	   desktop markup does not move. */
	@media (max-width: 620px) {
		.hero h1 br {
			display: none;
		}
		.hero h1 {
			font-size: 40px;
			line-height: 1.06;
		}
		.hero {
			display: flex;
			flex-direction: column;
		}
		.hero .free {
			order: 1;
			/* A flex item now, so it must not stretch to the column. */
			align-self: flex-start;
		}
		.hero h1 {
			order: 2;
			margin-bottom: 18px;
		}
		.hero .lede {
			order: 3;
		}
		/* The popover is a child component, so the parent's scope does not
		   reach it; :global does. It takes the place the plate had, under
		   the first paragraph; the plate follows the copy, as on wide screens. */
		.hero :global(.hero-popover) {
			order: 4;
			margin: 22px auto 4px;
		}
		.hero .lede2 {
			order: 5;
		}
		.hero .sub {
			order: 6;
		}
		.hero .cta {
			order: 7;
			margin-top: 22px;
		}
		.hero .brew {
			order: 8;
			align-items: flex-start;
		}
		.hero :global(.hero-plate) {
			order: 9;
			margin: 30px 0 4px;
		}
		.hero .stream {
			order: 10;
		}
	}

	/* Frames sit on ink — the one dark thing on the page, so they read as the
	   product rather than as decoration. */
	.stream {
		margin: 36px 0 0;
		background: var(--ink);
		border-radius: 14px;
		padding: 20px 22px;
		font-family: var(--mono);
		font-size: 13.5px;
		overflow-x: auto;
	}
	.stream .row {
		display: flex;
		gap: 15px;
		white-space: nowrap;
		padding: 3px 0;
		color: var(--stream-dim);
	}
	.stream .row .t {
		color: #ede4d6;
	}
	.stream .row .n {
		color: var(--terracotta-soft);
	}
	.stream .cap {
		color: var(--stream-dim);
		font-size: 12px;
		margin: 14px 0 0;
		white-space: normal;
	}
	.stream .cap .n {
		color: var(--terracotta-soft);
	}
	@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
		.r1 { animation: fade 0.5s both 0.1s; }
		.r2 { animation: fade 0.5s both 0.5s; }
		.r3 { animation: fade 0.5s both 0.9s; }
		.r4 { animation: fade 0.5s both 1.3s; }
		.r5 { animation: fade 0.5s both 1.7s; }
		@keyframes fade {
			from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(4px); }
			to { opacity: 1; transform: none; }
		}
	}

	tr.win td {
		color: var(--ink);
		font-weight: 600;
		background: rgba(196, 85, 46, 0.07);
	}
	tr.win td.num {
		color: var(--terracotta-ink);
	}

	.grid {
		display: grid;
		gap: 20px;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr));
		margin-top: 34px;
	}
	.card {
		background: var(--stone-deep);
		border-radius: 14px;
		padding: 22px 23px;
		border-bottom: 4px solid var(--stone-sink);
	}
	.card:nth-child(3n + 1) {
		border-bottom-color: var(--terracotta);
	}
	.card:nth-child(3n + 2) {
		border-bottom-color: var(--olive);
	}
	.card h3 {
		font-family: var(--sans);
		font-weight: 600;
		font-size: 16px;
		margin: 0 0 9px;
		color: var(--ink);
	}
	.card p {
		margin: 0;
		font-size: 14.5px;
		color: var(--ink-faint);
	}
	.fig-num {
		font-family: var(--mono);
		color: var(--terracotta-ink);
	}

	ul.uses {
		list-style: none;
		padding: 0;
		margin: 26px 0 0;
		max-width: 64ch;
	}
	ul.uses li {
		padding: 13px 0 13px 30px;
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--stone-line);
		font-size: 16px;
		position: relative;
	}
	ul.uses li::before {
		content: '';
		position: absolute;
		left: 2px;
		top: 22px;
		width: 13px;
		height: 13px;
		border: 2px solid var(--terracotta);
		border-left: 0;
		border-bottom: 0;
		border-radius: 0 12px 0 0;
	}
	ul.uses li b {
		color: var(--ink);
		font-weight: 600;
	}

	pre {
		background: var(--ink);
		border-radius: 14px;
		padding: 19px 21px;
		overflow-x: auto;
		font-family: var(--mono);
		font-size: 13.5px;
		color: #ede4d6;
		line-height: 1.75;
		margin: 24px 0 0;
	}
	pre .c {
		color: var(--stream-dim);
	}
	pre .k {
		color: var(--terracotta-soft);
	}

	.two {
